  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...No real changes to forecast thinking through \nSaturday as high pressure will gradually build overhead today \nbringing light and variable winds to the area before pushing \noffshore tonight. Could see a few isolated sea breeze showers \nthis afternoon and again on Sat afternoon but confidence is too \nlow to include any mentionable PoPs either day given ample dry \nair and subsidence over ENC. Otherwise seasonable temps forecast\ntoday as highs reach the upper 80s to low 90s inland, and mid \n80s along the coast. Lows tonight get into the upper 60s to 70s.\nSoutherly onshore flow begins in earnest on Sat allowing \natmospheric profile to gradually moisten. As a result while we \nlikely wont see much in the way of rain on Sat, we should see \nan increase in cloud cover, which will keep Highs in the upper \n80s to low 90s inland, mid 80s along the coast once again. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No real changes to forecast thinking as ENC once \nagain enters into a period of unsettled weather starting on Sunday. \nAn upper trough will be deepening over the Ohio Valley Sun morning \nas it moves towards the Eastern Seaboard, while at the surface \nan associated closed low is expected to traverse the Great Lakes\nregion. As these features move east, they will encounter the \nBermuda high which is expected to stall this upper trough, \nsurface low, and its associated fronts before they reach the \nEastern Seaboard. While the upper trough stalls, embedded \nshortwaves rounding the base of the trough are forecast to \nadvect into the region Sun through at least Mon/Tue. These \nfeatures, in addition to the sea breeze each afternoon/evening, \nwill be the main forcing present for a notable increase in \nprecip chances. \n\nThere will be two areas of focus for shower and thunderstorm \nactivity on Sunday. The first will be Sun morning along and behind a \nN'ward moving warm front/coastal trough with much of the activity \noccuring near and east of Hwy 17. The second will be in the \nafternoon/evening with shower and thunderstorm activity coming in \nfrom the west associated with the stalled upper trough. Cloud cover \nwill play a big role in how high instability can truly get, but with \nthe dewpoints rising there should be enough instability entering the \nregion for 1000-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE Sunday. While shear is not too \nimpressive with the unidirectional SE flow, the combination of long, \nskinny CAPEs, PWATs over 2\", and slow storm motions could result in \nheavy rainfall rates and wet microbursts where storms develop. Will \nnote shower and thunderstorm activity will be scattered in nature on \nSun so showers and storms will be \"hit or miss\". \n\nMonday the trough is still stalled to our west, but is just a little \nbit closer allowing for weak height falls and weak embedded \nshortwaves to move through aloft. This better forcing, paired with \nanother day of long skinny CAPEs and PWATs north of 2\" brings high \nprecip chances with heavy rainfall rates and a low end wet \nmicroburst threat and slow storm motion vectors. The better forcing \nlikely brings with it better coverage of precip. All this brings \nflash flooding concerns to mind, especially with how much rain has \nbeen observed in many locations in the past 1-2 weeks. We are \ncurrently in a Marginal (level 1/5) Excessive Rain Outlook for \nSunday. What prevents it from being higher is likely the uncertainty \non instability with antecedent cloud cover and the lack of \nsubstantial ground-level forcing. With high temps forecast to only \nbe in the low to mid 80s Sunday, the sea breeze may be weak in \nnature. \n\nBeyond Monday, the trough/low is still expected to linger to our \nwest until eventually it gets picked up and shifted to our north by \nanother trough moving through the Great Lakes region. As a result, \ndaily elevated precip chances continue through mid-week with a low \nend risk for flash flooding.",
